    "result_text": "The suite consists of the single `AcceptanceTest` class (with `AcceptanceBase` and `AuditLogCapture` as support), and it passes. The build is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe change: **on successful owner creation, emit an audit line via the `AUDIT` logger containing the owner id, customerCode, and registrationDate.**\n\nI implemented it keeping responsibilities cleanly separated:\n\n1. **New component `rest/audit/OwnerAuditLogger.java`** \u2014 a small `@Component` with a single responsibility: writing owner-lifecycle audit records to a dedicated logger obtained via `LoggerFactory.getLogger(\"AUDIT\")`. Its `logCreated(Owner)` method emits one line with `id`, `customerCode`, and `registrationDate`. Audit is a distinct concern from validation, so it lives in its own package rather than being bolted onto an existing validator or the controller.\n\n2. **`OwnerRestControllerV1`** \u2014 injected `OwnerAuditLogger` via the constructor and called `ownerAuditLogger.logCreated(owner)` in `addOwner(...)` right after the owner is persisted (so the id is assigned) and its customerCode/registrationDate are set. No business logic was duplicated or added to the controller itself.\n\nThe audit line reads e.g.:\n```\nAUDIT - Owner created: id=11 customerCode=TOW-SUR-0001 registrationDate=2026-09-16\n```\n\nThe `registrationDate` is logged from the same persisted `LocalDate` the GET endpoint returns (ISO `yyyy-MM-dd`), so the test's substring match against the read-back JSON succeeds. Full test suite: **Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0 \u2014 BUILD SUCCESS.**",
